---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Image from '../../components/OptimizedImage.astro';
import richardsonImg from '../../../assets/blog/Sweb_dad_1.jpg';

const blogPostingSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Building People, Not Just Buildings: A Community Spotlight on My Father, James Brinson Richardson",
  "description": "Community Spotlight on James Brinson Richardson — a lifetime of ministry in Shelby, NC, from Poplar Springs to Big Springs. A story of faith, family, and quiet leadership.",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Shelby Web Company", "url": "https://www.shelbywebco.com" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Shelby Web Company", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://www.shelbywebco.com/og-image.jpg" } },
  "mainEntityOfPage": "https://www.shelbywebco.com/blog/building-people-not-just-buildings-james-richardson-spotlight/"
};
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Building People, Not Just Buildings: A Community Spotlight on My Father, James Brinson Richardson"
  description="Community Spotlight on James Brinson Richardson — a lifetime of ministry in Shelby, NC, from Poplar Springs to Big Springs. A story of faith, family, and quiet leadership."
>
  <script is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingSchema)} type="application/ld+json"></script>
  <article class="pt-32 pb-24 px-4">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-16 text-center">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 mb-6 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-orange-500/20 bg-orange-500/5">
          <span class="text-orange-500 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">Community Spotlight</span>
          <span class="w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-orange-500/30"></span>
          <span class="text-brand-sky/60 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-widest">September 2026</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-heading text-5xl md:text-7xl font-black text-white mb-8 tracking-tight leading-[1.1]">
          Building People, Not Just Buildings <br />
          <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-brand-cyan to-brand-blue">A Community Spotlight on My Father, James Brinson Richardson</span>
        </h1>
      </header>

      <div class="mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto rounded-[2rem] overflow-hidden border border-white/10 max-h-[420px]">
        <Image src={richardsonImg} alt="James Brinson Richardson - Community Spotlight" width={800} height={600} class="w-full h-full object-cover object-top" />
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-invert prose-lg max-w-none">
        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          Every so often, a spotlight comes along that's harder to write than the rest — not because there's less to say, but because there's so much more of it, and none of it feels like enough. This is one of those. The subject of this week's Community Spotlight isn't a business owner or a public official. He's my father.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-16 mb-8">From Kentucky Roots to a Calling in Charlotte</h2>
        <p class="text-white mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          James Brinson Richardson was born in Kentucky in 1947 and raised in Charlotte, North Carolina. Charlotte is where he grew into the man who would go on to spend the rest of his life in service to others — first to his faith, then to his family, and then to an entire community that would come to think of him as their own.
        </p>

        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          By the early 1980s, my father had answered his calling and became the head pastor at Pleasant Plains Baptist Church. It was the beginning of a ministry that would eventually bring him — and our whole family — to Shelby, North Carolina.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-16 mb-8">Fifteen Years at Poplar Springs</h2>
        <p class="text-white mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          Not long after taking on that first pastorate, my father accepted the position of head pastor at Poplar Springs Baptist Church in Shelby, and that's the move that brought us to this community. He held that role for roughly 15 years, and in that time, the congregation grew to more than four times its original size.
        </p>

        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          My dad has always said it's not about the buildings — it's about the people. He didn't just grow a church; he built a family out of a congregation, one relationship, one visit, one Sunday sermon at a time. That distinction says everything about who he is.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-16 mb-8">Still Serving, Decades Later</h2>
        <p class="text-white mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          My father never really stopped. For the last 10 to 15 years, he's served as assistant pastor at Big Springs Baptist Church, and he stays active running interim pastorates — stepping in to guide congregations between pastors, offering steady leadership during a church's most uncertain seasons. It's the kind of quiet, behind-the-scenes work that doesn't come with a lot of recognition, but it's exactly the kind of work my father has always been drawn to. He shows up where he's needed.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-16 mb-8">The Man Behind the Pulpit</h2>
        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          Here's what I want people to know about my dad: he is, without question, the kindest, fairest, and most decent man I have ever known. That's not something I say because he's my father — it's something I've watched to be true my entire life, in the way he treats people, in the way he listens, in the way he never seems to run out of patience or grace.
        </p>

        <p class="text-white mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          He's been a preacher his whole life, but he's never been the stiff, serious type you might picture behind a pulpit. My dad loves to laugh. He's always got a joke ready, and he has a way of making a room feel lighter just by being in it. That sense of humor, paired with a deep and genuine faith, is part of what's made him so effective at what he does — people don't just respect him, they <em class="text-brand-sky/80">like</em> being around him.
        </p>

        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          And then there's my mom. My parents have been married for decades, and after all these years, they are still very much in love. Watching the two of them together — the way they still enjoy each other's company, still laugh together, still choose each other — has taught me more about love and commitment than anything I've ever read or been told.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-16 mb-8">A Life Spent Building People</h2>
        <p class="text-white mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          My father's story isn't really about titles or church growth statistics, even though both are part of it. It's about a man who has spent his entire adult life showing up for people — his congregations, his community, and his family — with humor, humility, and an unshakable kindness.
        </p>

        <p class="text-brand-sky/80 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
          Shelby has been shaped by a lot of good people over the years. My dad, James Brinson Richardson, is one of them. And I'm proud, more than words can really capture, to call him mine.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-orange-600 to-brand-darkest p-10 rounded-[2rem] text-center relative overflow-hidden my-12">
          <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[radial-gradient(circle_at_50%_50%,rgba(255,165,0,0.1),transparent_70%)]"></div>
          <p class="text-white text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium relative z-10">
            At the end of the day, this story isn't about churches or titles — it's about family. It's about a father who taught me what it means to lead with love, to serve without seeking credit, and to love your people well. That's the legacy I carry with me, and the one Shelby has been blessed to share. I love ya Dad.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 p-8 bg-brand-dark/50 border border-white/5 rounded-2xl">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">More Community Spotlights</h2>
        <div class="space-y-2 text-brand-sky/70">
          <p>Read more about the people and businesses keeping our region strong: <a href="/blog/support-first-responders-spotlight/" class="text-brand-cyan hover:underline">Supporting Local First Responders</a>, <a href="/blog/reflections-paint-body-works-spotlight/" class="text-brand-cyan hover:underline">Reflections Paint & Body Works</a>, and <a href="/blog/daniel-thomas-mayor-boiling-springs-spotlight/" class="text-brand-cyan hover:underline">Daniel Thomas, Former Mayor of Boiling Springs</a>.</p>
          <p class="mt-4">Love seeing local stories told? We spotlight Cleveland County businesses and leaders through our <a href="/services/web-design/" class="text-brand-cyan hover:underline">web design</a> and <a href="/services/seo/" class="text-brand-cyan hover:underline">local SEO</a> services.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <footer class="mt-24 pt-12 border-t border-white/5 flex flex-wrap gap-8 items-center justify-between">
        <a href="/blog/" class="text-brand-sky/60 hover:text-white transition-colors flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/></svg>
          Back to Blog
        </a>
        <div class="flex gap-6">
          <a href="/" class="text-brand-sky/60 hover:text-white transition-colors">Home</a>
          <a href="/about/" class="text-brand-sky/60 hover:text-white transition-colors">About Us</a>
          <a href="/contact/" class="text-brand-sky/60 hover:text-white transition-colors">Contact</a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>